From ee905e0e86e8137e70ba015308f34d66479b02b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aaron Scheiner <blue@aquarat.co.uk>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 18:46:38 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] avcodec/v4l2_m2m_enc: support B frames

The encoder forced V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_B_FRAMES to 0 and failed to open
if the driver kept a non-zero value, for want of DTS.

Ask for -bf B frames and use what the driver reads back. A stateful
encoder takes frames in presentation order and returns packets in decode
order, each with its own frame's timestamp, so the PTS are right and the
DTS have to be made: packet n gets the PTS of frame n - max_b_frames
(the first max_b_frames packets the first PTS minus whole frame
durations), which rises with n and never exceeds the packet's PTS for any
order the device codes a group of frames in. has_b_frames is set to the
same delay. Packets carry the key flag and, from the PFRAME/BFRAME
flags, the picture type in their quality stats (with the QP under -qp).

The dequeue logic had to change with it. receive_packet() blocked for a
packet after every frame, which deadlocks as soon as the device holds a
B frame until its reference arrives. It now queues the frame it has and
only waits for a packet when the device has more frames than it may hold
back (the B frames plus one being coded, which also overlaps the copy of
the next frame with the coding of the last); otherwise it returns EAGAIN
to get more input. Packets are copied out and their CAPTURE buffers
requeued at once, since a reordering device needs several of them per
group and a muxer may hold packets for a long time. A header-only packet
(HEADER_MODE_SEPARATE drivers) is put in front of the next one, so that
packets and frames stay one to one. An empty packet (a frame the device
failed) is an error. The EOS event no longer ends an encoder's drain
early: drivers may queue it before the buffer flagged LAST is done, which
dropped the last packets; the drain ends on that flag, and waits only a
bounded time once the event is in.

The DTS derivation is tested (fate-v4l2-enc-utils) against an emulation
of the Apple AVE driver's B-frame planner, for 0-2 B frames, GOP sizes
0-250, constant and variable frame rates, groups coded in any order and
drivers that code fewer B frames than they accepted, with a control that
must fail without the delay.

